---
// src/components/InBetweenSection2.astro
//
// Second "in-between" section — sits between EducationIslandSection
// and ArtIslandSection, per your request: "a new in-between section
// for the place between the education and art island section."
// Unlike InBetweenSection1.astro (a static photo + a dot-grid canvas,
// no motion in the background image itself), this one is built around
// a single looping "clouds" texture — you asked for it to "move the
// same speed as the clouds background for the artislandsection," so
// the clouds mechanism here is a direct copy of ArtIslandSection's own
// `.art-clouds-bg`/`.art-clouds-track`/`.art-clouds-tile` technique,
// not a new one: two identical `<img>` copies of `cloudsBackground`,
// side by side in a `width: max-content` flex track, `translateX`ed by
// exactly one tile-width per loop (`-50%` -> `0%`) so the seam between
// the two copies is invisible as long as the source image's own left
// and right edges already match (same requirement ArtIslandSection's
// clouds image has) — and the SAME `90s linear infinite` timing, so it
// drifts at the same visual speed. `your in_between_2_clouds_
// background.webp` happens to already be 7679x2160 — the exact same
// pixel dimensions as `art_island_clouds_background.webp` — so no
// extra scaling/cropping was needed to line the two up.
//
// No moon layer (this section doesn't have one), and no discrete
// foreground artwork/island either — this is purely a looping-sky
// backdrop, so there's only the one clouds layer plus an optional
// `<slot />` for text if you ever want to add any (same escape hatch
// InBetweenSection1.astro offers, unused by default).
//
// Overlap with EducationIslandSection (the section directly above
// this one in index.astro): per your request, "part of this
// background overlaps with the upper educationislandsection and its
// partly behind the education_island_ver3.webp." `.in-between-2-
// clouds-bg` below is NOT a plain `inset: 0` box like ArtIslandSection
// uses for its own clouds — its `top` is a NEGATIVE value (`-12vh`),
// so the box (and the looping tiles inside it) extends 12vh above
// this section's own top edge, physically reaching up into
// EducationIslandSection's box. Nothing clips that: no section in
// this codebase sets its own `overflow: hidden` (see the `overflow-x:
// hidden`/`clip` comment on `html` in Layout.astro) specifically so
// vertical bleed like this stays uncropped — the exact same page-wide
// rule that already lets islands overflow their own section's
// top/bottom edge is what makes this cross-section overlap possible.
// This section and EducationIslandSection both carry `.sky-chain`
// (see Layout.astro), so they already sit perfectly flush with zero
// gap between them — the `-12vh` overlap is layered on top of that
// flush seam, not compensating for a gap.
//
// Getting Education's building artwork to stay ON TOP of this
// overlap (rather than this section's clouds painting over the
// bottom of the building, which is what plain DOM order would do,
// since this section comes AFTER EducationIslandSection in the page)
// needed one small change over in EducationIslandSection.astro:
// `.education-image-wrap` there didn't have an explicit `z-index`
// before now — nothing forced it to, since it never needed to be
// compared against another SECTION's content, only against its own
// siblings inside `.education-visual`, where plain DOM order already
// put it above `.education-clouds-wrap`. But DOM order alone can't
// help across a section boundary in the OTHER direction: this section
// comes AFTER EducationIslandSection, so without an explicit z-index,
// normal paint order would put this section's overlap on TOP of
// Education's image, not behind it. Giving `.education-image-wrap` a
// `z-index: 1` (see its own comment there) — matching the SAME
// `z-index: 1` convention Layout.astro's `.halftone-spotlight`
// comment already documents for every other family member's own
// foreground artwork (`.art-island`, `.computer-island`, etc.) —
// settles it unconditionally: a positioned element with an explicit
// positive z-index always paints above a plain `z-index: 0`/`auto`
// sibling, regardless of which one comes later in the DOM. This
// section itself doesn't need any special z-index for that to work —
// only Education's own artwork needed elevating.
// `.in-between-2-section` DOES still need its own explicit z-index
// (alongside `position: relative`) for a narrower, purely LOCAL
// reason — the exact same one documented on `.in-between-section` in
// InBetweenSection1.astro: `.in-between-2-clouds-bg` below sits at
// `z-index: -1`, and without this section establishing its own
// stacking context, that negative z-index would escape past this
// section entirely and get compared against far-away content
// site-wide instead of just this section's own background. Any
// non-`auto` value establishes that local context — it was `0` when
// this section originally sat right after EducationIslandSection; now
// that it sits after ArtIslandSection instead, it's `-1` (see the
// "New" paragraph below for why the exact number had to change when
// the section's position on the page did). Either way, the point of
// this rule is the same: it keeps the whole `.in-between-2-section`
// subtree self-contained, so `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`'s `-1` only
// ever means "behind this section's OWN other content" (there isn't
// any right now besides the optional slot), not "behind some
// unrelated part of the page."
//
// A note on `in_between_2_clouds_background.webp` itself: you swapped
// in a new version of this image after the section above first
// shipped, and its own left and right edges no longer matched each
// other closely enough for the loop to read as seamless ("a ruff cut
// at the end and beginning of a new loop") — the two-tile `translateX`
// technique above only ever looks seamless if the source image's own
// edges already agree; it can't fix a source-level mismatch by itself.
// Measured directly (average of the 5 leftmost vs. 5 rightmost
// columns): the two edges differed by ~21 levels per channel on
// average, spiking to ~147 in places — a real, visible jump, not a
// rounding artifact.
//   Fixed by reprocessing the file itself, the same kind of source-
// level fix EducationIslandSection.astro's own clouds image needed
// earlier (see that file's history): rolled the whole image
// horizontally by exactly half its width so the mismatched edge-to-
// edge seam relocates to the dead center of the canvas, where there's
// full context on both sides to smooth it — then, in a narrow ~600px-
// wide band straddling that center point, cross-faded each column
// with its mirror partner on the opposite side of the seam (a true
// 50/50 average exactly at the seam, tapering by a cosine curve back
// to 0% blend — i.e. the untouched original — by the band's edges),
// and finally rolled the image back by the same half-width so the
// smoothed seam lands back at the image's own true left/right edges
// instead of sitting in the middle. A first attempt used a Gaussian
// blur instead of a mirrored cross-fade to smooth the seam — technically
// seamless (edges matched), but it visibly de-grained that one band,
// leaving an obvious smoother "stripe" standing out against this
// image's own fairly heavy film-grain texture everywhere else.
// Cross-fading with real (mirrored) image content instead of blurred
// content keeps the grain intact through the blend zone, so the seam
// no longer stands out either by color mismatch OR by texture
// mismatch. Re-measured after the fix: the same edge comparison above
// dropped to ~0.2 average, ~0.8 max — effectively identical, well
// under one level of typical 8-bit rounding noise. The vast majority
// of the image (everything outside that ~600px band) is pixel-for-
// pixel identical to what you uploaded; only the seam itself changed.
//
// New: this section originally lived between EducationIslandSection
// and ArtIslandSection, then got pulled out of the page entirely when
// you decided ArtIslandSection's own clouds bleeding upward achieved
// the same overlap more simply (see ArtIslandSection.astro's own
// history for that whole detour). It's now been put back on the page,
// but in a NEW spot — between ArtIslandSection and ComputerIslandSection
// instead. This component's own markup/CSS needed no changes for the
// move (there's nothing Education-specific baked into it, just a
// looping clouds section), except one: the z-index below.
//
// `.in-between-2-section` used to be `z-index: 0`, which was enough
// back when the section right before it (Education) had no explicit
// z-index of its own to compete with. ArtIslandSection, which now
// precedes this section instead, DOES have an explicit `z-index: 0`
// of its own — deliberately, so its own moon layer can bleed down
// past its bottom edge into whatever comes next (see the "Follow-up
// bug" / "Fix" paragraphs in ArtIslandSection.astro's history). With
// both sections tied at the same explicit `0`, the tie would break by
// DOM order — this section, coming after Art, would win and paint
// its own background over Art's bleeding moon, recreating the exact
// "cut off at the bottom" bug that was just fixed for
// ComputerIslandSection (see that file's own comment on
// `.computer-section`). So this section's z-index dropped from `0` to
// `-1`, the same fix, for the same reason: it still establishes its
// own local stacking context (any non-`auto` z-index does, negative
// included), so `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`'s own `-1` stays correctly
// scoped to just this section's own content — nothing about this
// section's INTERNAL layering changes. What changes is that Art's
// moon now wins outright (`0 > -1`) instead of losing a DOM-order tie,
// so it should bleed through into this section instead of getting cut
// off at the seam. (ComputerIslandSection's own `z-index: -1` fix is
// probably no longer doing anything now that this section sits
// between them and Art's bleed likely doesn't reach that far — no
// harm in leaving it as-is.)
//
// This section's OWN clouds still bleed 12vh upward past ITS top edge
// (`.in-between-2-clouds-bg`'s `top: -12vh`, unchanged) — that used to
// overlap with Education's building artwork specifically; now it'll
// overlap with the bottom of ArtIslandSection instead (its moon/clouds
// decorative background, not any foreground content, so nothing
// should visually clash) — untested visually since I can't preview
// the live page, so flag it if that seam looks off.
//
// Usage:
//   <InBetweenSection2
//     id="section-in-between-2"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/in_between_2_clouds_background.webp"
//   />
//   <!-- or, if you want to put something in it: -->
//   <InBetweenSection2
//     id="section-in-between-2"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/in_between_2_clouds_background.webp"
//   >
//     <p>Optional content here.</p>
//   </InBetweenSection2>

interface Props {
  id: string;
  cloudsBackground: string;
}

const { id, cloudsBackground } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="in-between-2-section sky-chain halftone-hover" id={id}>
  <div class="in-between-2-clouds-bg" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="in-between-2-clouds-track">
      <img class="in-between-2-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
      <img class="in-between-2-clouds-tile" src={cloudsBackground} alt="" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="content">
    <slot />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* An explicit z-index alongside `position: relative` is required,
     not decorative — see the top-of-file comment for why (keeps
     `.in-between-2-clouds-bg`'s negative z-index contained to just
     this section instead of escaping to compare against unrelated
     content elsewhere on the page). `-1` (not `0`) specifically so
     ArtIslandSection's own explicit `z-index: 0` — now the section
     immediately before this one — wins outright in their overlap
     zone, letting Art's moon bleed down into this section instead of
     this section's own background winning a DOM-order tie and
     covering it back up. */
  .in-between-2-section {
    position: relative;
    z-index: -1;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* No `aspect-ratio` dual-constraint here, unlike most of this
       family — that pattern exists elsewhere to guarantee a discrete
       illustration (a moon, a building) is never cropped top/bottom.
       This section's background is a seamlessly-tileable, endlessly-
       looping strip instead, so there's no "full picture" to protect;
       letting `cover`-style sizing (via the clouds tiles' own
       `height: 100%`) crop left/right on any viewport shape is
       exactly what a tileable texture is for. */
  }

  /* Full-bleed by default (an absolutely-positioned box with `inset`-
     style offsets is positioned against its containing block's
     PADDING edge, not its content edge — so this already ignores
     `.in-between-2-section`'s own left/right padding without needing
     the manual `left: 50%; width: 100vw; margin: -50vw` breakout trick
     EducationIslandSection's `.education-image-wrap` needs for its
     `position: relative` image; see that file's comment on why THAT
     one needs the extra trick and this one doesn't).
     `top: -12vh` (instead of a plain `inset: 0`) is the actual overlap
     mechanism — see the top-of-file comment for the full reasoning.
     `12vh` is a single easily-retunable number: raise it for more
     overlap into whatever section now precedes this one (Art, since
     the move — was Education), lower it (or drop to `0` for no
     overlap at all) if this turns out to be more than you wanted —
     I can't preview the live result myself. `overflow: hidden` clips
     the always-`max-content`-wide scrolling track back down to this
     box's own width, same as ArtIslandSection's `.art-clouds-bg`.
     `z-index: -1` sits it behind this section's own `.content` (in
     case you ever add any via the slot) — contained to just this
     section by `.in-between-2-section`'s own explicit z-index above
     (now `-1`, see that rule's own comment for why). */
  .in-between-2-clouds-bg {
    position: absolute;
    top: -12vh;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Two identical clouds tiles side by side, animated by exactly one
     tile-width via `transform: translateX` — the exact same technique
     ArtIslandSection's `.art-clouds-track` uses (see its own comment
     there for the full reasoning), copied here rather than shared,
     matching this codebase's existing convention of each section
     owning its own copy of whatever it needs. `width: max-content`
     sizes the track to exactly fit its two `<img>` children, so
     `translateX(-50%)` always shifts by precisely one tile's rendered
     width. Same `90s linear infinite` timing as Art's clouds, per your
     request that these move "the same speed." */
  .in-between-2-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    width: max-content;
    height: 100%;
    animation: in-between-2-clouds-drift 90s linear infinite;
  }

  /* Sizing purely by `height` (with `width: auto`) keeps each tile's
     full, uncropped width at its natural aspect ratio, so the image's
     own genuinely-matching left/right edges are what meet at the tile
     boundary — same reasoning as `.art-clouds-tile`. */
  .in-between-2-clouds-tile {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    max-width: none;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* FROM -50% TO 0% (not the reverse) reads as left-to-right drift,
     since the track's resting position places tile 1 in view and tile
     2 immediately to its right — identical to `art-clouds-drift`. */
  @keyframes in-between-2-clouds-drift {
    from { transform: translateX(-50%); }
    to { transform: translateX(0%); }
  }

  .content {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    margin: 0 auto;

    text-align: left;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /* Freeze the clouds on their resting frame instead of looping —
       matches ArtIslandSection's own reduced-motion behavior exactly
       (unlike EducationIslandSection's clouds, which deliberately stay
       animated regardless — see that file's comment on why; this
       section is modeled on Art's clouds, not Education's). */
    .in-between-2-clouds-track {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>
